WEBVTT

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.928
Being a human being is not easy as opposed to

00:00:04.928 --> 00:00:07.914
an animal because we're born and nobody gives us like

00:00:07.914 --> 00:00:10.916
a direction. Our parents might be a little bit, our

00:00:10.932 --> 00:00:14.930
college teachers, etc., mentors, but generally we're on our own.

00:00:15.845 --> 00:00:19.232
And it's a very, very difficult process. You wake up

00:00:19.296 --> 00:00:21.528
in the morning and you don't really know what you're—

00:00:21.528 --> 00:00:24.209
what you can do. You could choose 12 different paths.

00:00:24.273 --> 00:00:27.483
It can be very confusing and very overwhelming. When you

00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:29.726
find that sense of purpose, when you find what I

00:00:29.726 --> 00:00:33.539
call your life's task, everything has a direction. Everything has

00:00:33.587 --> 00:00:36.856
a purpose. Your energy is concentrated. It's not like you're

00:00:36.856 --> 00:00:40.237
just going down a single narrow pathway. It's not like

00:00:40.285 --> 00:00:43.282
life becomes boring and it's just about discipline and solving

00:00:43.346 --> 00:00:46.294
problems. It's actually the most exciting thing that can ever

00:00:46.294 --> 00:00:49.018
happen to you because you never have that lost feeling.

00:00:49.354 --> 00:00:50.893
You wake up in the morning and you go, yeah,

00:00:50.941 --> 00:00:53.280
this is what I need to accomplish. People come at

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.939
you with all kinds of Distractions and boring and irritating

00:00:57.036 --> 00:00:59.621
things, you're able to cut it out. It's just the

00:00:59.621 --> 00:01:02.560
most marvelous piece of internal radar that you can have.

00:01:03.009 --> 00:01:06.542
So I genuinely wish that everybody can find that kind

00:01:06.542 --> 00:01:07.891
of internal radar.

00:01:08.597 --> 00:01:11.985
And so it's not easy, and I understand that. There's

00:01:11.985 --> 00:01:15.486
no, like, instant formula, because we're all about instant formulas.

00:01:16.064 --> 00:01:18.071
It's difficult, and I want you to know that. So

00:01:18.087 --> 00:01:20.432
it's not like Robert can give me the answer in

00:01:20.496 --> 00:01:23.692
3 minutes. No, I can't. But there's a process involved.

00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:27.818
It's not, you know, a mystery. You can follow a

00:01:27.866 --> 00:01:29.231
very singular process.

00:01:29.954 --> 00:01:32.474
And the idea is, you were talking about childhood.

00:01:33.807 --> 00:01:36.184
The way I like to frame it is, when you

00:01:36.232 --> 00:01:40.262
were born, you are a phenomenon, you are unique. Your

00:01:40.326 --> 00:01:44.051
DNA has never occurred in the history of the universe,

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.476
going back billions of years. It will never occur in

00:01:46.508 --> 00:01:50.886
the future. Your life experience with your parents and everything

00:01:50.902 --> 00:01:53.227
that you experience in your early years going on up

00:01:53.483 --> 00:01:56.898
is unique. It's yours. You are one of a kind,

00:01:56.994 --> 00:02:00.825
right? So that is your source of power. To waste

00:02:00.857 --> 00:02:02.733
that is just the worst thing you can do in

00:02:02.765 --> 00:02:06.436
your life. And what, what the power is, is finding

00:02:06.468 --> 00:02:10.283
that uniqueness. What makes you you and how you can

00:02:10.299 --> 00:02:12.384
mine that and how you can go deep into it

00:02:12.464 --> 00:02:16.072
and use that. to create a career path, right? And

00:02:16.104 --> 00:02:18.896
so I tell people, when you're a child, when you're

00:02:18.896 --> 00:02:19.746
4 or 5

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.590
or even younger, you have what the great psychologist Maslow

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:26.323
called impulse voices.

00:02:27.077 --> 00:02:28.954
They're little voices in your head that say,

00:02:29.804 --> 00:02:32.740
I love this, I hate that, I like this food,

00:02:32.772 --> 00:02:35.178
I don't like when Mommy moves this way, I like

00:02:35.178 --> 00:02:36.317
when Daddy comes from here.

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:38.996
You're very cued into who you are and what you

00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:41.515
like and what you don't like. And these voices kind

00:02:41.515 --> 00:02:43.761
of direct you in certain ways, right?

00:02:45.029 --> 00:02:48.672
And when you're very young, they direct you towards intellectual,

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:50.052
mental pursuits as well.

00:02:50.967 --> 00:02:54.113
And there's a book I recommend for everybody. It's Howard

00:02:54.113 --> 00:02:57.852
Gardner's Five Frames of Mind. It's helped me immensely.

00:02:58.686 --> 00:03:02.522
The idea is he talks about 5 forms of intelligence.

00:03:03.437 --> 00:03:06.887
Our problem is we think of intelligence as mostly intellectual.

00:03:07.272 --> 00:03:10.317
But there are many forms of intelligence. There's the intelligence

00:03:10.317 --> 00:03:13.363
that has to do with words. There's abstract intelligence that

00:03:13.363 --> 00:03:16.889
has to do with patterns and mathematics. There's kinetic intelligence

00:03:16.889 --> 00:03:19.743
that has to do with the body. There's social intelligence.

00:03:20.079 --> 00:03:22.548
He has 5 of them. And the idea is

00:03:23.301 --> 00:03:26.747
your brain naturally veers towards one of them. It can

00:03:26.812 --> 00:03:29.505
veer towards 2 of them. That happens. But generally, one

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:31.204
of them kind of dominates, right?

00:03:31.941 --> 00:03:35.100
And it's like a grain in your brain. That's going

00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:37.458
in a certain direction. You want to go with that

00:03:37.491 --> 00:03:39.914
grain, and because that's where your power will lie.

00:03:40.636 --> 00:03:40.909
So

00:03:41.727 --> 00:03:43.942
when you're young, if you go back and think about

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:45.996
when you were 4 or 5, you can maybe get

00:03:45.996 --> 00:03:49.863
a picture of some kind of direction or voice inside

00:03:49.863 --> 00:03:53.057
of you that was impelling you towards this. I know

00:03:53.057 --> 00:03:53.618
for me,

00:03:54.421 --> 00:03:55.271
it was words.

00:03:56.747 --> 00:03:58.144
I can remember when I was 6 years old, I

00:03:58.176 --> 00:04:01.369
was just obsessed with words, just the letters in words.

00:04:01.786 --> 00:04:04.686
almost like in this almost slightly schizophrenic way. I would

00:04:04.686 --> 00:04:07.073
spell words backwards. I would take them apart. I would

00:04:07.073 --> 00:04:10.614
do anagrams. I love palindromes, right? So I had a

00:04:10.614 --> 00:04:13.306
thing about words and language. It's very primal.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.097
Some people, you know, Albert Einstein, when he was 4

00:04:18.129 --> 00:04:20.260
years old, his father gave him a birthday gift of

00:04:20.308 --> 00:04:20.837
a compass.

00:04:21.991 --> 00:04:25.099
And he was just mesmerized by this compass. The idea

00:04:25.099 --> 00:04:28.320
that there are invisible forces out there in the cosmos

00:04:28.384 --> 00:04:31.125
moving this needle. And he's obsessed with the idea of

00:04:31.125 --> 00:04:34.764
invisible forces. Steve Jobs, when he was like 7 or

00:04:34.796 --> 00:04:38.500
8 or maybe younger in Burlingame, California, his father— they

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:39.638
passed by a store with

00:04:40.584 --> 00:04:42.348
technological devices in the window,

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:45.683
and he was just hypnotized by the design of those

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.002
devices and the glass tubes and everything. So he wanted

00:04:49.002 --> 00:04:52.834
to go in that direction. Tiger Woods saw his father

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:55.511
hitting golf balls in the garage, and he was just

00:04:55.511 --> 00:04:58.333
like screaming with joy. He had to do that, right?

00:04:59.006 --> 00:05:01.235
You know, I could give you a million different examples

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:03.673
of this. Of course, these are people who are famous.

00:05:03.817 --> 00:05:07.441
Obviously, we can go back and find that. It's easier.

00:05:08.147 --> 00:05:10.408
But what happens to you— and please cut me off

00:05:10.408 --> 00:05:12.540
if I'm going on too long here. No, please continue.

00:05:13.118 --> 00:05:14.096
What happens to you

00:05:14.994 --> 00:05:16.934
is you're 7, now you're getting older,

00:05:17.848 --> 00:05:20.494
and you're starting to not hear that voice anymore. You're

00:05:20.494 --> 00:05:23.396
hearing the voice of your teachers telling you, you're not

00:05:23.396 --> 00:05:25.128
good at this field. You need to get better at

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:28.606
math. You know, you shouldn't be interested in these sports

00:05:28.606 --> 00:05:30.673
or anything. You should be going this way. Your parents

00:05:30.673 --> 00:05:32.981
are starting to tell you this is the career they

00:05:33.077 --> 00:05:34.920
want for you, the direction they want you to go

00:05:34.936 --> 00:05:35.352
in, right?

00:05:36.282 --> 00:05:38.942
You start hearing that more than your own voice. And

00:05:38.942 --> 00:05:41.426
as you get older, it gets worse and worse and

00:05:41.426 --> 00:05:44.823
worse. Then when you're a teenager, it's all about what

00:05:44.952 --> 00:05:47.740
other people are doing, your peers, what's cool, what's not

00:05:47.756 --> 00:05:50.528
cool, you know. And that kind of is more— So

00:05:50.624 --> 00:05:54.633
all of these Noise enters your brain and you can't

00:05:54.633 --> 00:05:56.365
hear that anymore. You don't know who you are.

00:05:57.135 --> 00:05:58.546
And so you go to college,

00:05:59.942 --> 00:06:00.904
you kind of maybe choose

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:02.604
a major

00:06:03.374 --> 00:06:05.907
that seems practical that your parents want you to go

00:06:05.956 --> 00:06:09.356
into. Maybe you kind of wander around, you're not sure.

00:06:09.949 --> 00:06:12.050
And then you enter the work world without that inner

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:15.482
radar that I'm talking about. And brother, you're lost, right?

00:06:15.963 --> 00:06:18.595
Where should I go? Well, I need to make money,

00:06:18.852 --> 00:06:19.189
right?

00:06:19.895 --> 00:06:22.159
And so you make a choice based on the need

00:06:22.159 --> 00:06:24.423
to make a lot of money. Some— not everyone, but

00:06:24.423 --> 00:06:25.307
some people do that.

00:06:26.158 --> 00:06:29.321
And I understand that need. We all need to make

00:06:29.321 --> 00:06:31.408
a living. But that can set you off on a

00:06:31.408 --> 00:06:33.046
very bad path because

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:37.076
you're not connected emotionally. The thing is, when you figure

00:06:37.076 --> 00:06:40.079
out that primal inclination, that grain that's inside of you,

00:06:40.849 --> 00:06:42.118
then you have

00:06:43.177 --> 00:06:47.462
the energy to be disciplined, to go through boring tasks,

00:06:47.526 --> 00:06:49.998
to learn. You learn at a faster rate because you're

00:06:50.014 --> 00:06:51.313
emotionally engaged.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:55.951
When you're emotionally engaged in a subject, the brain learns

00:06:56.112 --> 00:06:58.952
twice, 3 times, 4 times as fast as when you're

00:06:58.952 --> 00:06:59.305
not.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:01.472
I always give the example.

00:07:02.306 --> 00:07:04.938
In college, I studied foreign languages, which was kind of

00:07:04.938 --> 00:07:05.869
a passion of mine.

00:07:06.848 --> 00:07:09.078
For 3 or 4 years, I studied French.

00:07:09.913 --> 00:07:12.562
And then I went to Paris and I couldn't speak

00:07:12.562 --> 00:07:15.612
a word. It was useless because it didn't teach me

00:07:15.612 --> 00:07:19.465
anything practical, right? I was totally confused. And then, but

00:07:19.465 --> 00:07:21.102
I was in Paris and I loved it and I

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:22.692
wanted to live there, right?

00:07:23.542 --> 00:07:25.068
And I had a girlfriend and I needed to speak

00:07:25.068 --> 00:07:25.710
French to her.

00:07:26.609 --> 00:07:28.198
And I can tell you, in one month I learned

00:07:28.198 --> 00:07:31.601
more than those 4 years of university because I wanted

00:07:31.666 --> 00:07:34.796
to, because I was engaged, my emotions were there. It

00:07:34.796 --> 00:07:38.136
was like I had to survive. to learn French, whereas—

00:07:38.666 --> 00:07:40.737
so most of us, we don't have a need really

00:07:40.801 --> 00:07:44.158
to learn this subject. We're half— we're paying half attention.

00:07:44.832 --> 00:07:46.936
But when you find that thing that really connects to

00:07:46.936 --> 00:07:51.240
you, you're paying deep attention, your emotions are engaged, you're

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.596
learning at a much faster rate. Okay? And so

00:07:55.335 --> 00:07:57.326
the thing is, how do you find that when you're

00:07:57.422 --> 00:08:01.100
older? When you're 21, I give people a lot of

00:08:01.116 --> 00:08:03.380
help, and it's usually not so difficult. We can go

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:06.736
through that process. It gets harder when you're 30. and

00:08:06.736 --> 00:08:09.700
you've been wandering around, but it's not impossible. I didn't

00:08:09.716 --> 00:08:10.004
really

00:08:10.934 --> 00:08:14.811
start— find my exact path until I was 38, 39,

00:08:15.243 --> 00:08:18.336
to be honest. So there's hope. When you get 40

00:08:18.448 --> 00:08:20.611
and you get 50, it gets more and more difficult,

00:08:20.691 --> 00:08:23.751
right? And it's very sad if you wasted that seed

00:08:23.751 --> 00:08:26.699
of uniqueness that I'm talking about. And I tell people

00:08:26.699 --> 00:08:28.590
there are ways of going back, and we go through

00:08:28.622 --> 00:08:31.586
a process like archaeology. We have to dig and dig

00:08:31.586 --> 00:08:34.133
and dig and find those bones from your childhood that

00:08:34.133 --> 00:08:35.655
indicated what you were meant to do.

00:08:36.377 --> 00:08:39.267
But when you find your life's task, everything opens up.

00:08:39.621 --> 00:08:42.142
It doesn't mean you figured out, okay, I've got to

00:08:42.238 --> 00:08:44.903
aim for this particular job when I'm 28. That's not

00:08:44.903 --> 00:08:47.649
how it works. It gives you a sense of direction.

00:08:48.018 --> 00:08:50.587
You can try different things. You can experiment. You can

00:08:50.587 --> 00:08:52.691
have fun when you're in your 20s. You're going to

00:08:52.771 --> 00:08:54.987
learn. You're going to learn skills. But it gives you

00:08:55.003 --> 00:08:58.407
an overall framework instead of, whoa, all this confusion, this

00:08:58.471 --> 00:09:01.008
chaos, social media, the internet. I could go here, here,

00:09:01.024 --> 00:09:03.999
here. You're lost at sea. It gives you a very

00:09:04.015 --> 00:09:05.877
important sense of direction, a compass.

00:09:07.414 --> 00:09:10.924
As you describe this, I have this image of— you

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:11.844
mentioned animals

00:09:13.313 --> 00:09:16.412
that presumably don't have a lot of flexibility in terms

00:09:16.412 --> 00:09:18.172
of the niches they can exist in. But the way

00:09:18.253 --> 00:09:21.859
I imagine this process is that as a human, we're

00:09:21.875 --> 00:09:26.245
plopped into an environment— and here I'm using analogy— where

00:09:27.493 --> 00:09:29.946
We don't really know if we are an aquatic animal,

00:09:30.091 --> 00:09:33.900
a terrestrial animal, or an avian, right? Or an amphibian.

00:09:34.013 --> 00:09:35.337
Or an amphibian, for that matter.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:39.088
And to make the wrong choice, to be an amphibian

00:09:39.136 --> 00:09:41.671
who's trying to fly, although I'm sure they're out there

00:09:43.076 --> 00:09:44.061
in the animal kingdom,

00:09:45.202 --> 00:09:48.411
it's not just a waste of time, it's probably deadly.

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:53.103
And not to overdramatize the failure of finding one's purpose,

00:09:53.184 --> 00:09:54.953
but I see it that way. Whereas

00:09:56.269 --> 00:09:58.885
perhaps we could just say that the process of finding

00:09:58.901 --> 00:10:01.933
one's purpose is to realize, like, ah, I'm

00:10:02.848 --> 00:10:04.838
an amphibian. I can go in and out of water,

00:10:04.918 --> 00:10:07.261
whereas a bunch of other creatures around me stop at

00:10:07.261 --> 00:10:10.117
the water's edge. Right. Right. And this is really cool.

00:10:10.615 --> 00:10:12.524
And a bunch of these other things, like these flying

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:14.851
things, they can't actually even go in the water. Some

00:10:14.899 --> 00:10:16.760
of them might be on the surface or dive into

00:10:16.793 --> 00:10:18.814
it, but they can't do what I can do. So

00:10:18.895 --> 00:10:22.072
the process of self-discovery, it sounds like, is about restricting

00:10:22.169 --> 00:10:22.875
one's choices

00:10:23.727 --> 00:10:23.919
to

00:10:24.851 --> 00:10:28.014
a sort of wedge within the full landscape of options.

00:10:28.512 --> 00:10:29.476
And

00:10:30.359 --> 00:10:33.538
for me, I can certainly recall after reading Mastery, it

00:10:33.571 --> 00:10:37.585
helped me recall some early seed emotions that I experienced

00:10:37.762 --> 00:10:40.717
as a very distinct sensation in my body. Can you

00:10:40.717 --> 00:10:42.178
describe that? Yeah.

00:10:43.447 --> 00:10:45.390
Without making it too specific to my

00:10:46.418 --> 00:10:49.708
unique tastes, as a kid, I loved Flora and fauna.

00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:52.321
I love learning about biology. Sure. Yeah, no surprise there.

00:10:53.411 --> 00:10:56.377
But animals and how they move in particular, and fish

00:10:56.377 --> 00:10:59.567
and going to a proper aquarium store for the first

00:10:59.567 --> 00:11:01.812
time for me and going snorkeling for the first time

00:11:01.812 --> 00:11:04.698
was like, wow. And even as I describe it, it's

00:11:04.698 --> 00:11:06.622
almost like my body floats. I feel it in my

00:11:06.622 --> 00:11:09.139
left arm of all things. And it feels like there's

00:11:09.187 --> 00:11:11.944
something to do about it. It's not just that I'm

00:11:11.944 --> 00:11:14.557
in observation of things that delight me. Right. It's like

00:11:14.557 --> 00:11:17.283
there's something, there's an activation state created within me. Like,

00:11:17.299 --> 00:11:20.218
I gotta do something with this. And typically it's tell

00:11:20.266 --> 00:11:22.384
everybody about it until they won't listen anymore.

00:11:23.716 --> 00:11:26.380
But oftentimes it's to also draw those things, to think

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:28.386
about them, and I just delight in them. It's a

00:11:28.434 --> 00:11:31.146
constant source of delight. And so seeds such as those,

00:11:31.194 --> 00:11:32.494
and there are a few other things in that

00:11:33.344 --> 00:11:35.735
landscape of flora and fauna and

00:11:36.457 --> 00:11:39.057
learning about animals and biology,

00:11:39.763 --> 00:11:43.052
including the human animal, and then organizing information feels so

00:11:43.052 --> 00:11:46.518
satisfying to me. It's like a drug that— And so

00:11:46.518 --> 00:11:47.673
it just feels like this

00:11:48.731 --> 00:11:49.758
eternal spring of

00:11:50.897 --> 00:11:53.527
life, right? And so for me, that's what it was.

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:56.991
And in 2015, when I was teaching that course, the

00:11:56.991 --> 00:11:58.755
course I loved, but I was feeling a little bit

00:11:58.755 --> 00:12:02.027
astray in my scientific career. And then I read Mastery

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:05.572
and I realized, yes, I love running a laboratory, I

00:12:05.572 --> 00:12:08.458
love teaching, but there's something else for me. Right. And

00:12:08.458 --> 00:12:10.656
it has to do not with a podcast. I didn't

00:12:10.656 --> 00:12:12.725
even know what a podcast— I knew what a podcast

00:12:12.725 --> 00:12:14.521
was. I was listening to podcasts at that time,

00:12:16.493 --> 00:12:18.338
But I wasn't on social media. I had no thoughts

00:12:18.338 --> 00:12:20.262
of having a podcast. But what I wanted was that

00:12:20.262 --> 00:12:21.801
feeling in its

00:12:23.084 --> 00:12:25.618
total number of forms. That's the goal. Get that feeling

00:12:25.618 --> 00:12:28.312
in as many forms as possible. Right. Is that about—

00:12:28.504 --> 00:12:31.134
That's absolutely perfect because

00:12:31.872 --> 00:12:35.400
the connection to what I'm talking about, it's not an

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:38.912
intellectual thing. It's visceral. It's emotional. It's physical.

00:12:39.665 --> 00:12:41.445
Right. And you feel it in your body. And when

00:12:41.494 --> 00:12:44.603
you're doing it, It's like it's at your level. It's

00:12:44.699 --> 00:12:47.487
like you're swimming with the current. You feel it. Things

00:12:47.535 --> 00:12:51.461
are easy. Everything clicks together. There's a delight. Not everything

00:12:51.509 --> 00:12:53.704
is going to be delightful. There's going to be tedium

00:12:53.753 --> 00:12:56.637
involved. There's going to be moments of boredom. But you're

00:12:56.717 --> 00:12:59.409
able to withstand the moments of boredom because you feel

00:12:59.938 --> 00:13:04.024
that deep overall connection. So yes, that's precisely what I'm

00:13:04.024 --> 00:13:04.392
talking about.

00:13:05.226 --> 00:13:07.421
I mean, it's— for me, it's a little bit— a

00:13:07.469 --> 00:13:10.516
similar thing is I said about words, but the other

00:13:10.516 --> 00:13:12.395
thing that I was obsessed with when I was a

00:13:12.507 --> 00:13:12.732
kid

00:13:13.631 --> 00:13:15.863
was early human ancestors.

00:13:16.618 --> 00:13:19.653
Don't ask me why. I just was so obsessed with

00:13:19.653 --> 00:13:23.185
our ancestors millions of years ago and how it's possible

00:13:23.522 --> 00:13:25.963
to be living here in the '60s or '70s with

00:13:25.995 --> 00:13:28.837
cars and everything, but to come to where we are

00:13:28.837 --> 00:13:29.014
now.

00:13:30.266 --> 00:13:33.509
And I wrote a short story when I was 8

00:13:33.558 --> 00:13:37.493
years old. about a vulture. It was written from the

00:13:37.493 --> 00:13:40.160
point of view of a vulture watching the first humans

00:13:40.224 --> 00:13:42.634
kind of emerge on the planet. I'm sure it was

00:13:42.859 --> 00:13:46.940
absolutely awful, dreadful. But the weird thing is, I'm writing

00:13:46.972 --> 00:13:48.835
a new book, and all I'm doing in that book

00:13:48.835 --> 00:13:51.968
is going into early humans. And I feel like a

00:13:52.033 --> 00:13:54.844
kid again. I'm so excited. I'm so happy. So I

00:13:54.844 --> 00:13:56.418
can very much relate to your story.

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:00.997
You mentioned these 5 different forms of intelligence or frames

00:14:01.029 --> 00:14:02.406
of mind. as you referred to them.

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:04.464
And

00:14:05.629 --> 00:14:06.568
I'm certainly aware that

00:14:07.393 --> 00:14:10.953
I lean towards a more intellectual interest, although, as you

00:14:11.131 --> 00:14:14.100
pointed out, the excitement, the delight is visceral. Yeah. And

00:14:14.116 --> 00:14:17.057
the actions are actions. They're of the body, ultimately. Right.

00:14:17.571 --> 00:14:20.624
One has to draw, speak, write books, et cetera,

00:14:21.347 --> 00:14:23.832
to transmute that excitement into something real.

00:14:25.369 --> 00:14:26.567
For people that

00:14:27.565 --> 00:14:30.881
are not as intellectually tuned, but maybe are kinesthetically tuned,

00:14:30.946 --> 00:14:31.399
for instance.

00:14:33.064 --> 00:14:34.294
I can only wonder what that's like.

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:37.032
I'm not completely uncoordinated, but I don't think I have

00:14:37.097 --> 00:14:41.458
a kinesthetic attunement or frame of mind. But I, for

00:14:41.506 --> 00:14:45.259
instance, had a podcast listener mention

00:14:46.014 --> 00:14:46.288
that they

00:14:47.380 --> 00:14:50.336
think in feels, that they literally

00:14:51.188 --> 00:14:55.539
experience thought as sort of a patchwork of bodily sensations.

00:14:55.655 --> 00:14:59.083
Right. Right. And that thought for them is not of

00:14:59.292 --> 00:15:01.827
the stuff from the neck up, but only

00:15:02.565 --> 00:15:04.876
from the neck down, which to me was really intriguing.

00:15:05.454 --> 00:15:06.321
And so I only

00:15:07.059 --> 00:15:09.642
raise this because there have to be, as you point

00:15:09.642 --> 00:15:12.900
out, there's an infinite number of different sort of orientations

00:15:13.173 --> 00:15:15.131
based on our unique DNA and experience. But

00:15:16.302 --> 00:15:17.040
what do you think

00:15:18.164 --> 00:15:22.352
explains why these particular seeds, or as you point out,

00:15:22.802 --> 00:15:24.855
the direction that the grain runs in the brain. I

00:15:24.887 --> 00:15:27.248
mean, it's partially going to be nature, it's going to

00:15:27.248 --> 00:15:28.444
be DNA. For sure.

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:32.804
But we're talking about this as if there's some exciting

00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:35.237
or awe-inspiring or delightful

00:15:36.043 --> 00:15:37.139
thing that captures us.

00:15:37.977 --> 00:15:39.606
Can it be the other way, too? Can it be

00:15:42.012 --> 00:15:44.354
one has a bad experience as a child in an

00:15:44.354 --> 00:15:46.438
intellectual environment and then decides,

00:15:49.008 --> 00:15:51.815
things of the body feel good, things of the mind,

00:15:51.896 --> 00:15:54.800
of intellect, feels bad. And does it matter whether or

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:58.560
not we are drawn to our purpose by recognizing what

00:15:58.560 --> 00:15:59.029
we love

00:15:59.789 --> 00:16:03.332
or what we hate, or are both useful? Oh, they're

00:16:03.365 --> 00:16:04.724
both very, very useful.

00:16:06.961 --> 00:16:08.220
A lot of intelligence

00:16:09.317 --> 00:16:14.094
is nonverbal. We think in terms of images. We're very

00:16:14.094 --> 00:16:17.200
much infected by the emotions of other people. So I

00:16:17.265 --> 00:16:18.167
know, for instance,

00:16:18.969 --> 00:16:22.789
My mother is very, very interested in history. She's obsessed

00:16:22.789 --> 00:16:27.155
with history. And I probably absorbed her interest in history.

00:16:27.283 --> 00:16:30.205
I don't think there's a genetic gene for that interest,

00:16:30.253 --> 00:16:33.158
you know? So you're going to absorb things from your

00:16:33.158 --> 00:16:36.754
parents as well. So it's not all just genetic. But

00:16:36.754 --> 00:16:39.338
yeah, what you hate will pay a big— will have

00:16:39.338 --> 00:16:42.035
a big thing. But the problem with doing that is

00:16:43.046 --> 00:16:46.240
if you go into a direction and you're in elementary

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:49.033
school, etc., and they force you to learn math and

00:16:49.033 --> 00:16:51.118
you hate it, What it tends to do is it

00:16:51.118 --> 00:16:52.834
turns you off from learning in general.

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:55.704
You think, I don't want to, I don't want to,

00:16:55.881 --> 00:16:57.308
I don't want to be disciplined. I don't want to

00:16:57.308 --> 00:17:00.017
go through anything because it's painful. It doesn't lead anywhere.

00:17:00.098 --> 00:17:02.872
It's not me. It's frustration. It turns you off from

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:06.527
learning in general. So it's really, really important for a

00:17:06.527 --> 00:17:10.359
child to have the love experience as early as possible

00:17:10.712 --> 00:17:12.652
so that they can know what they hate and why

00:17:12.845 --> 00:17:15.362
they hate it. Right. And then they can rebel and

00:17:15.362 --> 00:17:18.297
they can go into that field as opposed to I

00:17:18.313 --> 00:17:21.010
hate learning. I hate discipline. I hate studying. I hate

00:17:21.123 --> 00:17:24.495
trying things over and over again. If you're kinesthetically oriented,

00:17:25.137 --> 00:17:27.610
and a part of me, I understand that because I

00:17:27.610 --> 00:17:28.268
love sports,

00:17:29.215 --> 00:17:31.800
is you have to practice. It's going to take a

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:34.626
lot. You're not going to instantly be good at something,

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:37.324
right? And that's going to require a love of it,

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:41.402
right? But if your math experience is, I hate learning

00:17:41.418 --> 00:17:44.581
shit, it's going to transfer to sports. You're going to

00:17:44.645 --> 00:17:48.404
hate discipline in general. So it's very important for parents

00:17:48.404 --> 00:17:51.505
to let that child have at least glimmers of that

00:17:51.633 --> 00:17:52.292
love moment.

00:17:53.063 --> 00:17:53.818
I know for me,

00:17:54.605 --> 00:17:54.879
when I

00:17:55.955 --> 00:17:57.787
finished college and I entered the work world,

00:17:59.024 --> 00:18:00.775
I had to get a job. I got worked in

00:18:00.775 --> 00:18:02.414
journalism. I hated it.

00:18:03.458 --> 00:18:06.494
I hated working for other people. I hated office politics.

00:18:06.559 --> 00:18:10.077
I hated all the egos. I hated the smarminess. I

00:18:10.109 --> 00:18:13.001
hated the lack of quality. It was all just about

00:18:13.627 --> 00:18:15.153
making money and getting things out there.

00:18:15.891 --> 00:18:18.604
And then I worked in Hollywood. I hated Hollywood. I

00:18:18.604 --> 00:18:22.553
hated working in Hollywood. That formed me very much, made

00:18:22.553 --> 00:18:24.415
me go in the direction that I went in, but

00:18:24.624 --> 00:18:26.872
only from the basis of I knew that I wanted

00:18:26.872 --> 00:18:29.954
to be a writer. So that's very important that it's

00:18:30.034 --> 00:18:33.389
not just hate. It can form you, but there also

00:18:33.389 --> 00:18:37.162
has to be that positive, deep emotional love of something

00:18:37.162 --> 00:18:39.666
that also is grounded in you in some way.

00:18:40.837 --> 00:18:43.465
What you just said really highlights the fact that energy

00:18:43.961 --> 00:18:47.150
and motivation can come from either pressure,

00:18:48.063 --> 00:18:51.300
desire for something, or desire to get away from something.

00:18:51.572 --> 00:18:54.344
Right. And earlier, when you were talking about

00:18:55.258 --> 00:18:56.491
how we are so much more

00:18:57.517 --> 00:19:02.693
engaged and driven towards things that stir us emotionally, and

00:19:02.869 --> 00:19:05.609
actually, we know, based on the neuroscience, as you know

00:19:06.009 --> 00:19:07.083
too, I'm sure, that

00:19:08.093 --> 00:19:10.690
Only by the release of certain neurochemicals in the brain

00:19:10.738 --> 00:19:13.126
and body would our brain have any reason to change,

00:19:13.207 --> 00:19:15.515
right? If you don't feel agitation and you can do

00:19:15.531 --> 00:19:17.279
everything that you're trying to do, of course your brain

00:19:17.295 --> 00:19:20.661
wouldn't change. Like, why would it, right? That agitation is

00:19:20.661 --> 00:19:23.883
a signature of the neurochemicals that are saying, hey, something's

00:19:23.883 --> 00:19:24.717
different now, right?

00:19:25.471 --> 00:19:28.036
You might need to do something different, including rewire yourself,

00:19:28.196 --> 00:19:28.452
right?

00:19:29.334 --> 00:19:32.637
And that can come from positive or negative experiences. Of

00:19:32.637 --> 00:19:32.749
course.
